<header>
  <p class="venue">CV4Ecology 2026 · Fish Species Recognition Challenge · Codabench 16815 · Technical Report</p>
  <h1>Blind open-set recognition of 17,393 fish species under fine-grained, long-tailed and zero-photograph conditions</h1>
  <p class="authors"><b>Lalith Sai</b> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Cheng Yaw Low</b></p>
  <p class="affil">Intelligent Systems Laboratory (ISLab), Changwon National University, Republic of Korea
    &nbsp;·&nbsp; System: <span class="n">FishONet&nbsp;v109</span>
    &nbsp;·&nbsp; <a href="https://github.com/lexus-x/FihOnet">github.com/lexus-x/FihOnet</a></p>

  <div class="result">
    <div class="big"><span class="n">53.736%</span><span class="l">overall</span></div>
    <div><span class="n">77.61%</span><span class="l">photographed</span></div>
    <div><span class="n">22.91%</span><span class="l">no photograph</span></div>
    <span class="sp">official leaderboard · one uniform rule<br>no split membership used</span>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="abstract">
  <h4>Abstract</h4>
  <p>The CV4Ecology 2026 challenge asks a single classifier to assign each of 35,665 evaluation
  photographs to one of 17,393 fish species. Three difficulties compound rather than merely coexist.
  The task is <b>fine-grained</b> — congeneric species differ by pigment pattern and fin detail, not
  by shape; severely <b>long-tailed</b> — the median photographed species carries two training
  images and 79.4% carry five or fewer; and <b>open-set</b> — 11,598 of the 17,393 species (66.7%)
  have no training photograph anywhere and exist only as a text description and a position in the
  taxonomy.</p>
  <p><b>Our implementation follows the competition rules exactly, and we ship the checker that
  proves it.</b> The rules make the seen/unseen split protocol-only: a system may not determine
  whether an evaluation image belongs to a photographed species, nor restrict its candidates on that
  basis. We never use that information. One uniform rule applies to all 35,665 images; every
  prediction is a single argmax over the full 17,393-class space; no fish-specific model or dataset
  is used; every external resource is disclosed. A released audit script re-verifies each claim
  mechanically (§2) — including the positive evidence that our router is blind: it reaches only
  89.11% / 77.58% route purity where a forbidden folder oracle would score 100% / 100%.</p>
  <p>FishONet reaches <b>53.736% overall</b> on the official leaderboard — 77.61% on photographed
  species, 22.91% on species with none — from a 45.39% closed-set baseline across 109 numbered
  builds. Our most transferable finding is methodological: on this task the magnitude of a holdout
  gain is not evidence. We document a validation pool that produced a +13.374-point proxy gain worth
  +0.006 real points, the leak that caused it, and the redesign that turned the same idea into
  +0.213.</p>
</div>

<main>

<h2><span class="num">1</span>The problem</h2>

<p>Each evaluation image receives one species from a fixed vocabulary of 17,393, scored on plain
overall accuracy across 35,665 images. Because 20,097 of those images (56.35%) belong to species
that appear in training and 15,568 (43.65%) do not, accuracy decomposes exactly as</p>

<p style="text-align:center;margin:1.0mm 0 1.4mm"><span class="n">overall = 0.5635·A<sub>seen</sub>
+ 0.4365·A<sub>novel</sub></span></p>

<p>This identity governs every trade-off that follows: a point on the novel side is worth 0.77 of a
point on the seen side, and an image sent to the wrong side scores zero with certainty.</p>

<h3><span class="num">1.1</span>Three problems at once</h3>

<p><b>Fine-grained.</b> Sibling species within a genus share body plan, posture and habitat,
separating only on pigment pattern, stripe count and fin morphology. Generic visual features are
close to useless here — which is why we build on organismal pre-training rather than a
general-purpose vision–language model (§5.4).</p>

<p><b>Long-tailed.</b> The 64,259 labelled training images spread across 5,795 species at an
imbalance ratio of 140:1. Both ends are extreme: 289 species (5%) hold 69% of all images, while the
median species has <b>two</b>, 79.4% have five or fewer, and 90.6% have ten or fewer. There is
almost no per-class signal to fit for most classes, so the seen route is built on prototypes and
nearest exemplars rather than a learned classifier head.</p>

<figure>
  <img src="paper_assets/task.png" alt="Training photographs per species, ranked">
  <figcaption><b>Figure 1. All three difficulties in one distribution.</b> Training photographs per
  species, ranked, log scale, read directly from <code>label_train.json</code>. The teal curve is
  the long tail; it bottoms out at two images and stays there for half the photographed species. The
  clay bar is the open-set problem: 11,598 species for which no photograph exists at all.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p><b>Open-set.</b> Two-thirds of the label space is unphotographed. Those species are reachable
only through text — the provided descriptions and the taxonomy — or through images of them found
outside the competition data. This is not rejection against a background class: every one of them is
a scoreable answer, so the system must actively retrieve them.</p>

<p>The three interact. Long-tailedness is what makes the fine-grained problem hard — two images
cannot characterise a species that differs from its sibling by a stripe — and it also softens the
open-set boundary, since a class with two training photographs is barely better resourced than one
with none.</p>

<h2><span class="num">2</span>The rules, and how we comply</h2>

<p>The competition forbids using the seen/unseen split at inference. <code>splits/test.pkl</code>
and <code>splits/unseen.pkl</code> are protocol-only: a system may not read them to learn whether an
evaluation image is of a photographed species, and may not restrict a prediction to one side of the
split. A folder-oracle router would be trivially strong, and is explicitly disallowed.</p>

<p>This single constraint shapes the architecture. The seen/novel decision must be made
<em>blind</em>, from the image and the class texts alone, before any candidate set exists — and
every error in it is unrecoverable, because the route chosen cannot contain the true label. §6.2
shows this costs about 15.9% of all images and sets the design's ceiling.</p>

<p>We treat compliance as something to demonstrate rather than assert.
<code>research/verify_compliance.py</code> ships with the code, re-runs every claim below, and exits
non-zero on any failure. It deliberately reads the split files — which no builder does — in order to
prove the router could not have been an oracle.</p>

<table>
  <caption><b>Table 1.</b> Every check re-run against the submitted artefact; all pass.</caption>
  <tr><th>Claim</th><th>Machine-checked evidence</th></tr>
  <tr><td>No split file read at inference</td><td class="d">static scan, 6/6 builders in the chain clean</td></tr>
  <tr><td>One argmax over 17,393</td><td class="d">21,399 predictions photographed, 14,266 unphotographed</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Router is blind</td><td class="d">89.11% / 77.58% route purity; an oracle scores 100 / 100</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No fish-specific model</td><td class="d">5 general organismal backbones; 2 fish fine-tunes declined</td></tr>
  <tr><td>External data disclosed</td><td class="d">iNat Open Data, TreeOfLife-200M; FishBase unused, declared</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Gate artefact reproducible</td><td class="d"><code>learned_gate_v79.pkl</code> rebuilds bit-identically</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The route-purity figure is the load-bearing one. Were we consulting the split files, every
seen-split image would reach a photographed class and every unseen-split image an unphotographed
one. Ours reach them 89.11% and 77.58% of the time: the system makes exactly the errors a blind
system makes, and anyone holding the prediction file can check it.</p>

<h3><span class="num">2.1</span>The one gray zone, measured rather than argued</h3>

<p>Five points in the pipeline couple across the evaluation batch: member and gate standardisation,
the <span class="n">f</span>-quota rank cutoff, <code>dbnorm</code>'s across-image term, and Sinkhorn
on the novel route. The rules forbid split-based routing; they do not mention batch-coupled
inference either way. Rather than argue the reading, we built the strict alternatives and measured
what they cost. Freezing the two <em>routing</em> couplings against holdout-calibrated constants
changes <b>729 of 35,665</b> predictions (2.04%); additionally freezing <code>dbnorm</code>'s hub
term changes 4,213 (11.81%). Both builds ship, so organisers can select a strictness level and
re-run rather than accept or reject the submission whole — and the licence carries an explicit
carve-out granting them the right to inspect, execute and reproduce all of it.</p>

<h2><span class="num">3</span>Experimental setup</h2>

<p>Training data is the provided 64,259 labelled images over 5,795 species, plus
<code>descriptions.json</code> covering all 17,393 classes. Evaluation is the provided 35,665-image
test set, scored on overall accuracy with seen and novel returned as diagnostics. The budget of 3
submissions per day and 30 in total is why so much of this report concerns predicting a real gain
before spending a slot on it.</p>

<p><b>Domain shift.</b> Evaluation photographs are framed differently from training ones: mean
width-to-height aspect ratio <span class="n">2.12</span> against <span class="n">1.15</span> — wide
specimen-style crops versus roughly square citizen-science captures. All fine-tuning therefore uses
shift-matched augmentation (<code>RandomResizedCrop</code>, <code>scale (0.35, 1.0)</code>,
<code>ratio (0.5, 2.0)</code>), and the novel route pools over seven crops per image. This shift is
also what degrades the gate at test time (§6.2).</p>

<p><b>Compute and external resources.</b> All training and inference ran on a single NVIDIA L40S
(46 GB). We use no model or dataset built for fish recognition; every backbone is a general
organismal or general-purpose foundation model. External resources, fully disclosed:
<b>iNaturalist Open Data</b> (117,225 research-grade images covering 7,364 species, the novel
route's photo bank) and <b>TreeOfLife-200M</b> embeddings, merged into the BioCLIP-2 prototype
legs.</p>

<figure class="span">
  <img src="paper_assets/pipeline.png" alt="FishONet v109 inference pipeline">
  <figcaption><b>Figure 2. The v109 inference pipeline.</b> One uniform rule over every evaluation
  image. The gate sees only signals computed from the image and the class texts — never split
  membership — and assigns a quota rather than thresholding. Both routes are scored and the label is
  one argmax over all 17,393 classes, so a prediction is never restricted to one side.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2><span class="num">4</span>Implementation</h2>

<h3><span class="num">4.1</span>Encoders</h3>
<p>Five encoders, all fine-tuned only on the provided training images: three BioCLIP-2.5 ViT-H/14
variants (<code>ctftshift</code>, contrastive LoRA with taxonomic context; <code>ftshift</code>; and
<code>fullft336shift</code> at 336 px), one BioCLIP-2 ViT-L/14 with a frozen backbone plus a LoRA
adapter merged with TreeOfLife-200M representations, and TaxaBind ViT-B/16 as a taxonomic anchor.</p>

<h3><span class="num">4.2</span>Seen and novel routes</h3>
<p>For a candidate among the 5,795 photographed species the score is a weighted sum of mean class
prototype, nearest training exemplar, and taxonomic text similarity, weights
<span class="n">(1.0, 2.5, 2.5)</span>. The exemplar term dominates because of the long tail: at a
median of two images per class a class mean is a poor summary, and one well-matched exemplar is the
stronger evidence. For the 11,598 unphotographed species, ten legs are fused under a debiased
normalisation (<code>dbnorm</code>) that removes each leg's per-class score bias: six text legs
(multi-template taxonomy, common names, taxonomic context across four encoders), two iNaturalist
photo-bank legs with 7-crop max-pooling, and two BioCLIP-2 prototype legs merged with
TreeOfLife-200M.</p>

<h3><span class="num">4.3</span>The quota gate</h3>
<p>Routing is a ranking problem, not a thresholding problem. All 35,665 queries are scored by a
12-feature logistic model — seen-side maximum similarity, top-1/top-2 margin, log-sum-exp entropy
gap, per-encoder maxima, the seen-text-minus-novel-text margin, and photo-bank confidence — and the
top <span class="n">f = 0.60</span> quantile routes to the seen head. A quota is invariant to the
uncalibrated scale drift the domain shift induces, and is uniform by construction: the rule depends
on nothing about an image but its rank.</p>

<h3><span class="num">4.4</span>Leak-free re-ranking, genus backoff</h3>
<p>The top <span class="n">K</span> candidates per route (10 seen, 20 novel) are re-ordered by a
logistic re-ranker, 32 features seen-side and 34 novel-side. Every feature is a within-query
statistic — percentile rank, gap-to-maximum, per-leg z-score — hence invariant to candidate-pool
size; §5.3 explains why that invariance separates a real gain from an artefact. The final build adds
one seen-route signal: per candidate, <code>ctftshift</code> prototype and exemplar evidence pooled
across sibling species of the same genus (<span class="n">λ<sub>G</sub> = 3.0</span>). It changes
312 of 35,665 predictions (0.87%) and leaves the gate bit-identical.</p>

<h2><span class="num">5</span>Ablation analysis</h2>

<h3><span class="num">5.1</span>New evidence compounds, re-weighting does not</h3>

<p>The gate produced the project's largest single gain, and decomposing it twice yielded the most
useful rule we found. A gate change moves overall accuracy through two channels: the <em>routing</em>
term (how many images reach a route that can contain their label) and the <em>conditional</em> term
(how accurate each route is on what it receives).</p>

<table>
  <caption><b>Table 2.</b> Two changes to the same gate, decomposed. Real leaderboard deltas.</caption>
  <tr><th>Change to the gate</th><th class="r">Routing</th><th class="r">Cond.</th><th class="r">Real Δ</th></tr>
  <tr><td>v56→v77 &nbsp;2 features → 12<br><span style="color:#8C8C8C">new information</span></td>
      <td class="r">+1.082</td><td class="r">+0.686</td><td class="r"><b>+1.767</b></td></tr>
  <tr><td>v77→v79 &nbsp;same 12, C = 1 → 100<br><span style="color:#8C8C8C">re-weighted only</span></td>
      <td class="r">+0.481</td><td class="r" style="color:#8F2D2D">−0.439</td><td class="r">+0.042</td></tr>
</table>

<p>New features change <em>which</em> images sit near the boundary, and since they also predict
whether the receiving head will be correct, both terms rise together. Re-weighting the same features
only slides marginal images across a fixed ordering: routing improves, the conditional term falls
almost as much, and 90% of the gain is returned. We thereafter projected every gate proposal at 0.1×
its routing delta and stopped tuning. The genus backoff was accepted on the same logic —
sibling-species similarity is information absent from the base fusion — and also transferred
positively, making the rule 2 for 2 on real submissions.</p>

<h3><span class="num">5.2</span>The operating point</h3>

<p>The quota <span class="n">f</span> trades the routes against each other at roughly 4:1. Three
real submissions bracket it and the response is concave, so <span class="n">f = 0.60</span> is a
measured optimum rather than a tuned guess. Holdout is <em>anti-correlated</em> on this axis and was
not used for it.</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Quota</th><th class="r">f = 0.55</th><th class="r">f = 0.60</th><th class="r">f = 0.65</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Real overall</td><td class="r">53.520</td><td class="r"><b>53.697</b></td><td class="r">53.492</td></tr>
</table>

<h3><span class="num">5.3</span>A validation leak, and what a proxy point is worth</h3>

<p>Our first shortlist re-ranker (v81) scored <b>+13.374</b> on a class-disjoint 5-fold
cross-validated pseudo-novel holdout and <b>+0.006</b> on the leaderboard. The cause was the pool,
not the model. Gold labels in that holdout are always drawn from the 1,159 rarest <em>training</em>
classes — only 9.1% of the 12,757-class candidate pool, and a subpopulation identifiable from the
features. The re-ranker learned to prefer gold-eligible classes, picking one 53.7% of the time
against the fusion's 38.4%, rather than to rank evidence. Class-disjoint cross-validation cannot
detect this: the leak is in the pool's composition, not in the class partition.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <span class="lab">The rule we now apply</span>
  <p>Per-<em>image</em> decisions such as the gate are safe to train on the holdout.
  Per-<em>candidate</em> learning — re-rankers, learned fusion weights, calibrators over the class
  axis — must be trained on a pool in which every candidate is gold-eligible, with
  pool-size-invariant features. The magnitude of a holdout number is not evidence; the population it
  was measured on is.</p>
</div>

<p>Rebuilding the pool that way (v82) <em>lowered</em> the proxy gain to +8.154 while raising the
real gain to <b>+0.213</b>. The seen head gave a consistent exchange rate, and that anchor —
<span class="n">0.026–0.030</span> real points per leak-free proxy point — became our instrument for
killing proposals cheaply: a full point of accuracy needs +34 proxy points, which nothing
approached.</p>

<table>
  <caption><b>Table 3.</b> The same idea, measured on a leaking pool and on a clean one.</caption>
  <tr><th>Re-ranker</th><th>Candidate pool</th><th class="r">Proxy Δ</th><th class="r">Real Δ</th></tr>
  <tr><td>v81 novel</td><td class="d">leaky, 9.1% eligible</td><td class="r">+13.374</td><td class="r fg">+0.006</td></tr>
  <tr><td>v82 novel</td><td class="d">leak-free</td><td class="r">+8.154</td><td class="r"><b>+0.213</b></td></tr>
  <tr><td>v83 seen</td><td class="d">leak-free</td><td class="r">+1.803</td><td class="r">+0.053</td></tr>
</table>

<h3><span class="num">5.4</span>Encoder choice, and what we closed</h3>

<p>Roughly 25 backbones were screened on the novel route. Organismal pre-training dominates: BioCLIP
reached 21.53% where SigLIP2 reached 5.13% and BioTrove ≈0%. No general-purpose vision–language
model was competitive, and no encoder-side lever survived after v56 — every later real gain came
from evidence the pipeline already computed.</p>

<p>Directions we closed, so they need not be re-run. <b>Re-ranker model class is inert</b> —
gradient boosting against logistic regression on clean pools gives −0.008 novel, +0.003 seen; the
ceiling is set by the features, not the learner. <b>Gate recalibration is exhausted</b> — six
levers, holdout AUC pinned at 0.981, no real gain beyond v79. <b>Transduction gives little</b>:
Sinkhorn over the evaluation batch was worth +0.09 (v31 47.69 → v33 47.78) and turned negative in
the final system. <b>Multi-crop TTA</b> on one ensemble member changed 0.6% of predictions, and
<b>recover-on-eject</b> (v34) lost 0.07.</p>

<h2><span class="num">6</span>Results</h2>

<h3><span class="num">6.1</span>The campaign</h3>

<p>Every number below is a real leaderboard score, not a holdout estimate. The system went from
45.39% to 53.736% over 109 numbered builds; the novel route carried almost all of it, 8.51% to
22.91%, while seen accuracy stayed inside a two-point band.</p>

<figure>
  <img src="paper_assets/campaign.png" alt="Real leaderboard accuracy across the build campaign">
  <figcaption><b>Figure 3. What 109 builds bought.</b> Upper strip: every numbered build, the 13
  measured on the leaderboard in black — a budget of 3/day and 30 total is why most were resolved
  offline. Lower: real overall accuracy at each measured milestone.</figcaption>
</figure>

<table>
  <caption><b>Table 4.</b> Measured leaderboard progression. Seen and novel columns are the
  organiser-reported diagnostics.</caption>
  <tr><th>Build</th><th class="r">Seen</th><th class="r">Novel</th><th class="r">Overall</th><th>What changed</th></tr>
  <tr><td>v22</td><td class="r">—</td><td class="r">—</td><td class="r">45.390</td><td class="d">closed-set shift-robust ensemble</td></tr>
  <tr><td>v33</td><td class="r">78.20</td><td class="r">8.51</td><td class="r">47.780</td><td class="d">single-pipeline routing baseline</td></tr>
  <tr><td>v36</td><td class="r">78.95</td><td class="r">10.42</td><td class="r">49.040</td><td class="d">shift LoRA + TaxaBind leg</td></tr>
  <tr><td>v56</td><td class="r">75.67</td><td class="r">20.57</td><td class="r">51.616</td><td class="d">7-crop max-pool, 336 px bank</td></tr>
  <tr><td>v77</td><td class="r">77.72</td><td class="r">21.96</td><td class="r">53.383</td><td class="d">learned 12-feature gate</td></tr>
  <tr><td>v82</td><td class="r">77.45</td><td class="r">22.91</td><td class="r">53.644</td><td class="d">leak-free novel re-ranker</td></tr>
